title: skgrepo-sets
pid: 0f501e3e-a31b-45f0-83ca-aa95d5398992
body: |2-
    - Skgrepo-sets are the prefixes of the config's privacy ladder, the
      owned skgrepos (see "skgconfig.toml > skgrepo-sets" below): each
      owned skgrepo's name denotes itself plus every more public owned
      one. The skgrepo restriction is two things: one such rung, the
      most private owned skgrepo shown, and whether the foreign
      skgrepos are shown ("shown" unless a connection hides them). Its
      set is the rung's prefix, plus every foreign skgrepo when they
      are shown. A connection that shows the config's most private
      owned skgrepo and the foreign skgrepos is unrestricted.
    - Request: ((request . "list repo sets"))
    - Response: LP response-type "repo-sets" with
      =((restriction "NAME") (foreign-repos "shown"|"hidden") (sets
      ("NAME1" "NAME2" ...)))=. The sets are the owned skgrepo names in
      privacy order (most public first), the last of them no skgrepo
      restriction when foreign skgrepos are shown; order is meaningful
      (it is the privacy ladder), so clients should preserve it.
    - Request: ((request . "skgrepo restriction"))
    - Response: LP response-type "skgrepo-restriction" with
      =((restriction "NAME") (foreign-repos "shown"|"hidden"))=.
    - Request: =((request . "set skgrepo restriction") (name . "NAME")
      (foreign-repos . "shown"|"hidden") (approved-overPrivateText-pids
      "PID" ...))=, NAME the most private owned skgrepo to show. Either
      of =name= and =foreign-repos= may be left out, and then stays as
      it is; one of them must be present.
    - Response: Multiple length-prefixed messages, sent sequentially:
      1. Skgrepo-set response:
         =Content-Length: N\r\n\r\n((response-type skgrepo-restriction) (restriction "NAME") (foreign-repos "shown"))=
      2. The rerender stream, as in "rerender all views":
         rerender-lock → rerender-view* → rerender-done. Each open view
         is re-rendered in place under the new set by the refresh alone:
         every occurrence of a node the new set keeps out becomes, in
         place, a restricted viewnode where a record the set shows names
         it, keeping its viewchildren, and vanishes elsewhere, with its
         subtree; a member whose record alone the new set keeps out
         vanishes too, where its parent reads claims; an editable
         folder whose every member's record the new set keeps out stays,
         empty, as any empty editable folder does; and the refresh fills
         every folder per the new set, so a skgrepo widening makes each
         restricted viewnode an occurrence again and draws again the
         members and folders it reveals. A view root the new set keeps
         out stays, as a restricted viewnode, whether or not a record
         names it. The folders under an occurrence that became a
         restricted viewnode become tombstones, holding their members,
         and stay tombstones after a skgrepo widening. None of this is
         reported: the response names the new skgrepo restriction, and
         nothing more (see "Restricted-skgrepo nodes" below).
    - Behavior: The skgrepo restriction is per TCP connection. It
      defaults from =skgconfig.toml=, resets on reconnect, and is not
      written back to the config. Changing it requires confirmation in
      either client, and re-renders (not closes) all active Skg buffers for that
      connection. The rerender starts from each view as the server last
      stored it, and the clients replace each buffer's text with the
      result, which would lose an unsaved edit; so each client refuses
      the switch while any view has unsaved edits ("Save or revert
      every skg view before switching the skgrepo restriction"), before
      it sends the request. The server's own refusals are the two
      below.
    - Refusal: the endpoint's error paths (neither a name nor
      =foreign-repos=; a =foreign-repos= other than "shown" or "hidden";
      or a name that names no owned skgrepo of the config, a foreign one
      included: "'NAME' names no owned skgrepo of the config. The
      skgrepo restriction is the most private owned skgrepo to show;
      foreign skgrepos are shown or hidden apart from it.") answer in
      the normal skgrepo-restriction response-type,
      carrying the explanatory text as =content= and the unchanged set
      as =restriction=, followed by an empty rerender stream (a
      rerender-lock naming no views, then rerender-done), so the
      client's preemptive buffer locks and its stream-in-progress guard
      unwind.
    - Rerender authorization precedes both the set change and release of
      view text. If the proposed set would render an overPrivateText telescope, the
      first reply is LP =overPrivateText-telescope-confirmation= with
      =(operation repo-set-switch-rerender)= and exact PIDs, followed by an empty
      rerender stream. A retry adds =approved-overPrivateText-pids=; declining
      leaves the skgrepo restriction and open-view registry unchanged.
